  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread rain tonight into Monday will help \nease ongoing drought conditions in some areas. Rain may be heavy\nat times tonight into Sunday, especially across Downeast Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA surface low pressure system developing off the Mid Atlantic \ncoast this morning will progress north today into the Gulf of\nMaine. As a warm front lifts north ahead of the low expecting\noverrunning rainfall to develop into the region late this\nafternoon into tonight. The CAMs are starting to hone in on the\nbest path of the steady rain and a good timing. Expect rain to \nbegin along the coast late afternoon into the evening and push \nNNE through tonight. The question for tonight will be how much \nrainfall will occur, especially for areas in the north. \nUnfortunately, there remains significant differences in the \nensembles as of this update. Models do agree the PWATs tonight \nwith this surge of moisture on the warm front increases to \n1.3-1.8 inches which is 140-180% of normal. Potential exists for\n0.5-1 inch of rain tonight in Washington County with \nsignificantly less rainfall north and west. However, confidence \nin low to moderate on where the heaviest rain will be. Any \nrainfall will be helpful to ongoing drought conditions given \nthat we will be in a cloudy cool pattern for the next couple \ndays. This will allow for rainfall to get into the soils, \nunlikely to help streamflows which are significantly below \nnormal. \n\nAfter midnight, the center of the low will be over \nDowneast and becomes absorbed by the approaching occluded front\nfrom the west and a vertically stacked low pressure system. The\nlow centered over the Great Lakes will progress E over New \nEngland. By Sunday, expect widespread showers continuing \nthroughout the day. Modeled PWATs increase to 1.8-2.2 inches \nsince we will be within a warm sector and significant moisture \nadvection from the tropical Atlantic. Given the 160-180% of \nnormal PWATs (95-99th percentile) and showers may produce heavy \nat time rainfall. Showers will be more convective during the day\nso uncertain where the beneficial rainfall will occur. Then \nthroughout Sunday night, the low becomes stationary over Quebec \nas it rotates the moisture through the state. By Monday, the low\nbegins to gradually move north which will decrease rain chances\nthroughout the day. However, best chance of showers and \nthunderstorms will be in the northern 1/2 of the CWA closer to \nthe vertically stacked low in Quebec.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper level low pressure keeps the chance of \nshowers going into midweek, especially across Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThere remains blocking in the North Atlantic (-NAO) into the end\nof August. Upper level low pressure to our north in Quebec\ncontinues to slowly drift east towards the Maritimes. Partly to\nmostly sunny skies with cool pool aloft on Tuesday will lead to\nsignificant cu build up especially in the northern 2/3rd of the\nCWA. Expecting some daytime instability, around 500j/kg of\nSBCAPE, so widespread showers and some thunderstorms are \nexpected to develop. Very little if any showers expected south\nof The Airline (Route 9). The upper level low north of the Gaspe\nPeninsula on Wednesday will reduce the precip chances to just\nnorthern Maine and mostly dry elsewhere with highs getting to\n2-5 degrees above normal for this time of year.",
